In article <41su5n$jm8@geraldo.cc.utexas.edu>,
yschoe@schoch.che.utexas.edu (Yoonsuck Choe) writes:

:I am having a problem with my DEC alpha 3000/400 system.
:
:It suddenly went down last night, and displayed the console
:prompt >>>. It says that somekind of bus error is coming up.
:Is this a serious hardware error? If not, is there any way to 
:solve it? Please take a look at the attached system status,
:and please help me out. Thanks.

<snip>

:And the "show device" results in:
:
:	Devnam			Devstat
:	------			-------
:	SCSI			??	130	0008

  The above does not appear to be the output from an UNIX/OpenVMS PALcode
  >>> SHOW DEVICE command.  (It does look like it might be part of the
  output from a SHOW CONFIG command.)

:	T-STS-SCSI B - OK
:	?? 130 SCSI 0x0008

  `?? 130 SCSI 0x0008' indicates that FRU 130 displayed error 8 during
  the SCSI test.  I don't have the service manual handy for this box and
  thus cannot decode FRU 130 down to the failing FRU part for you.  And
  based on some guessing and on the TEST SCSI output, it looks like the
  SCSI-A bus is the bus on which the problem(s) are occuring.

  If you've checked all enclosures for correct (short) SCSI cabling and
  for the presence of the SCSI terminator, etc., and you've power-cycled
  the system and all expansion enclosures -- power up the system enclosure
  last -- and you still get the error, you will want to contact the local
  system hardware support folks.  (I would personally check and/or reseat
  the SCSI cabling and terminator only with power off to all enclosures.)


  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
