ehrhart.jeffrey@mayo.edu (Jeffrey T. Ehrhart) wrote:

> I am having a problem with the DEC Alpha C++ fstream
> functions.  My code looks like this:
> 
>   xfile.open("test.txt", ios::in);
>   char delimiter[6];  
>   xfile.getline(delimiter, 7); // Read first 6 characters
>   xfile.seekg(0);  // Reset to file beginning
>   xfile.sync();       // Sync file and fstream buffer
> 
> The problem is that the next getline invocation does
> not read from the beginning of the file like I wanted
> it to.  It appears as if either the seekg or sync 
> functions did not work as I expected.  Do I need to
> do something different to move the file pointer back
> to the beginning of the file?  Is it a problem with the
> DEC C++ library functions?  HELP !!!
> 
> This code works as expected under Visual C++ on
> the PC and the CC compiler on a SUN.

Caution: I know nothing, I'm just reading the manual.

The VC++ help for getline says:

Extracts characters from the stream until either the delimiter delim
is found, the limit nCount-1 is reached, or end of file is reached.
The characters are stored in the specified array followed by a null 
terminator. If the delimiter is found, it is extracted but not stored.

Therefore, you're reading 6 characters, but storing 7 characters (your
6 plus a '\0') in the 6 character array. Maybe the '\0' is overwriting
something it shouldn't? The fact that it works elsewhere may be luck.

Or, you could try tellg before the getline, or cast 0 to streampos?

PJDM
--
Peter Mayne
Digital Equipment Corporation (Australia)
Canberra, ACT
----
These are my opinions, and have nothing to do with Digital.
The truth is out there, but not necessarily in here.




