organization: UMR Missouri's Technological University
newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.hardware,comp.sys.sun.misc,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.misc,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.networking,comp.sys.ibm.pc.hardware.systems,comp.client-server

Marshall Wang (mjwang@megazone.tti.com) wrote:

: 	I am setting up a generic file server system in a mixed platform
: environment, and I need more information about the following machines:

: 		Sun SPARCstation  5 85MHz / Sun OS
: 		Sun SPARCstation 20 50MHz / Sun OS
: 		Pentium	100/120/133MHz / Windows NT
: 		DEC Alpha

: 	What are the strength and weaknesses of each machine?  (Speed is a
: major factor to consider.)

	For a _file_ server, you can drop CPU speed by quite a bit - if 
you spend the extra money on RAM, RAM, RAM, good (fast and reliable, with 
solid drivers) ethernet devices, and good (see above) drive controllers.  
RAID is a nice, but expensive, things to have.  Above all, more RAM is 
better, no matter what the platform.

	Also, if you want a _file_ server, I wouldn't recommend Win NT, 
personally - it's designed more as an applications server for PC clients 
which run Windows (Microsoft, not X).  If most of your clients are PC's, 
personally, I recommend Netware 3.12 or 4.1, depending on your needs.  If 
most of your clients are Unixen, I prefer a Sun Sparcserver with NFS - 
Sun has very good NFS implementations, with local drive caching, and a 
few other nice bells and whistles.

	Basically, pick the machine for the job.  If you want the machine 
to serve both PC clients and Unixen, I might suggest splitting the 
project into two specialized machines (and probably seperate the 
networks, as well, with a bridge or router or group thereof).  If you 
also want an application server, you may want to consider a third 
machine, each specialized for the job at hand.  
						Patrick Hall
