In article <407ulq$akd@ixnews7.ix.netcom.com>, dpaz@ix.netcom.com (David Pazuchanics ) writes:
#I asked about this a while back, but didn't get much of a response.  If
#someone knows this to be impossible, please let me know.
#
#I would like to send mail from Node A that can only do SMTP to node C
#that can only to VMS mail.  In between I have Node B that has DEC
#TCP/IP Services for VMS (aka UCX) version 3.1.  I can currently do the
#following:
#
#SMTP from A to B:       user_B@node_B
#SMTP from B to A:       smtp%"user_A@node_A"
#VMS mail from B to C:   node_C::user_C
#VMS mail from C to B:   node_B::user_B
#
#Now, how can I address mail on Node A so that it gets to Node C?  How
#do I need to set up UCX's SMTP configuration on Node B so that this
#happens?  I'm currently using BIND.  Do I need to add some sort of MX
#records?  C'mon guys and gals, some one out there must be doing this.

If you were using MultiNet, you could do something like:

  SMTP%"'node_c::user_c'@node_b"

or, again, using MultiNet, setup your DECnet mail domain to be 
decnet.companyname.com, and then you could setup an MX record like:

  node_c in mx 10 node_b

and then send mail from node A to node C using:

  SMTP%"user_c@node_c.decnet.companyname.com"

... more complicated methods exist, dependin on exactly what sortof
work you want to do, and what you want your users to do.

-Dan Wing, wing@tgv.com



