Message-ID: <40vsmc$3pv@lll-winken.llnl.gov>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 108351514
references: <40q6qo$jb6@grape.epix.net>
organization: Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In Article <40q6qo$jb6@grape.epix.net>
dpeasley@news.epix.net (Async User) writes:
>I have three VAX 8800 systems and one VAX 6440 which all use the DEBNA
>addapter for network traffic.  All systems are running VMS.  I zero the
>NCP line counters each night, but every day these four systems get over
>1000 errors in the 'SYSTEM BUFFER UNAVAILABLE' entry.  The NCP manual
>says that it can be any!!! buffer between the BNA and the user buffers.
>
[...snip...]
>
>Is there a mod or upgrade to the DEBNA adapters ???

System buffers are not adjustable, so there is little you can do except change
hardware. "System buffer unavailable" simply means that data is coming into the
unit faster than the unit and it's driver can process it. The DEBNA is faster
than the DEBNT, but pretty slow by current standards.

Upgrading the DEBNA to a DEBNI (this is a microcode change on the board and not
too expensive last I checked) will significantly improve the poor DEBNA
performance. It fixed the problem on our 6330.

R. Kevin Oberman			
