Steve Spurlock (spurlock@ix.netcom.com) wrote:
>I need information for a VT like terminal to be able handle being in a
>harsh magnetic environment that measures about 200 gausse

>would a liquid crystal display work?

>i am looking for a low cost solution.  any information on who makes a
>vt terminal to work in such an environment would be appreciated.

>we have tried insulating a normal DEC terminal and putting the terminal
>in different enclosures.  no matter what we try the screen gets highly
>distorted.

Here at TRIUMF (a very large cyclotron partical accelerator facility) 
we us a lot of CRT terminals in high magnetic field environments.  They 
have all been adjusted to compensate for the magnetic fields; mainly I 
think the yokes have been twisted.  This is not a perfect solution, however 
it does improve things a lot.  Some degree of sheilding also helps.  When 
the magnets are shut down, all the displays go on an angle, and visitors 
think everyone who works here are twisted (partly true).  Also, the 
compensation applied by twisting the yoke is only valid for a specific 
location and orientation of the display.  We live with it.

The small 2-line X 20 character LCD panels do not seem to be affected 
in the high magnetic fields.

Hope this helps.

--  rod.

