In article <40vjqs$uku@classic.iinet.com.au> richardp@iinet.com.au (Richard Paton) writes:
>I was wondering if anyone knows what the future of the kernel of OSF/1 
>is?  In particular, I understand it is presently some unholy aliance 
>between BSD-type drivers and Streams-type drivers.  Our problem is that 
>we will be writing our own protocol drivers, and it seems that the way to 
>interface with the ethernet drivers is using the packet-filter driver 
>(sorry if that's not the exact name; I'm at home and don't have the 
>details on me).
>
>Also, we may want to suck the bottom of the IP layer and shove it down 
>our stack;  that apparently has to happen through the ipnet (?) driver.  
>Our drivers (by the way) are Streams drivers.

If you want to use Streams, then the packet filter is not for you.
It's a character-device driver, neither sockets-based nor streams-based.
Not quite what the standards bodies like, but it's quite fast.

As one of the main authors of the original packet filter code, I have
some bias in its favor.  It lets you send and receive any Ethernet
(or FDDI) packets you want, without having to fight other parts
of the protocol stack that insist on using their own packet formats.
On the other hand, you have to implement all the layers yourself.
It's a tradeoff.

>My question is how stable is all this.  Are we likely to lose all these 
>'facilities' in the (near|far) future?

Digital's policy seems to be to announce all "features retirements"
at least several releases in advance.  (Sorry, I don't know the
actualy official policy, but it's something like that.)  So far, we
seem to be much better at adding features than at removing them!
In general, the Digital UNIX people try quite hard to maintain
full binary-level compatibility across releases, wherever possible.

I am reasonably certain that they have not made any retirement announcement
about either Streams, or BSD sockets, or the packet filter.  

I also have not seen any suggestions from inside the Digital UNIX
product group that anyone even wants to retire any of these interfaces,
although I imagine that some of the engineering managers would have
easier lives if they had to support fewer features.

If you need more specific assurances (such as, "Digital promises not
to remove feature X before year Y") then you will need to talk to
the Digital UNIX management types directly.  But I would be quite
surprised to see any of these facilities removed any time soon.

And anything  that is an important part of the X/Open UNIX specification 
is going to be in Digital UNIX for a long time, because otherwise they won't
let us call it "UNIX."

