In article <41c9jf$ktr@loke.btj.se>, goran@loke.btj.se (Goran Svensson) writes:

|>I have a UNIX program to read and write ANSI labelled tapes, and VMS indeed
|>does create ANSI labelled tapes when doing a copy. But, VMS uses the
|>possibility to add system specific headers, HDR3 and HDR4, which is not a
|>part of the standard. Is there anyone who knows the meaning of the fields?
|>And, if I have a standard ANSI labelled tape, what does VMS think of them ?

HDR3 contains VMS RMS file attributes for the file, 32 bytes of binary data
encoded as 64 hexadecimal ASCII characters.  For UNIX I think you can ignore
these (I don't know the individual meaning of the various bytes).

HDR4 contains the remainder of a VMS filename that wouldn't fit in HDR1.

VMS can handle ANSI tapes without HDR3 and HDR4 just fine - VMS maps the ANSI
file characteristics onto their VMS counterparts.
