ron@topaz.sensor.com (Ron Natalie) wrote:
>Eh?  Our Alpha motherboards come with Pentium DOS bios.  When the AXP
>processor is used, the ROM is ignored.
>
You must have a AlphaStation 400 which uses a motherboard designed for 
486 and Pentium and therefore has a x86 BIOS on it. That same motherboard
also accepts an Alpha daughter card which has the console EPROMs for 
Alpha.

Other AlphaStation motherboards only have the Alpha console EPROMs 
capable of running all 3 OSes. All the AlphaStations to date can run any 
of the 3 OSes. Windows NT uses the ARC console (originally defined for 
ACE/MIPS), while Digital Unix and OpenVMS use a different console called 
the SRM console.

It is possible for someone who wants to support NT only, to provide 
support for only the ARC console. BTW, the console includes an x86 
emulator so they can run BIOS code on PCI adapter cards. I don't know 
what console code is provided by the third party motherboard providers.





