In article <40c5ab$er4@caip.rutgers.edu>, halasz@caip.rutgers.edu writes:
>Why is it a good idea that there is this message,
>	%SYSTEM-x-NORMAL, normal successful completion
>which is not meaningful unless x is "S"?

This is the single case where this is true. Obviously, there has to be
some status that says everything is normal.

>Why is it a good idea that there is this message,
>	%SYSTEM-x-ABORT, abort
>which is not meaningful unless x is "E" or "F"?

This is not true. There are many reasons why one might want to return
different levels of "abort":

   exit(41)    %SYSTEM-S-ABORT, abort   The user requested an operation
                                        be aborted rather than exiting
                                        normally, This was handled
                                        successfully.

   exit(43)    %SYSTEM-I-ABORT, abort   Same as above, but since the
                                        abort is not normally used we
                                        flag it for information.

   exit(40)    %SYSTEM-W-ABORT, abort   Everythings's probably OK, but
                                        the user should be warned that
                                        aborting is not recommended

   exit(42)    %SYSTEM-E-ABORT, abort   The program should not have
                                        been aborted. Something is wrong.

   exit(44)    %SYSTEM-F-ABORT, abort   Something is seriously wrong!

>For file not found, even "S" can be useful.

No problem...

   exit(98961) %RMS-S-FNF, file not found

Of course, since the routine to find a file is looking for a named file,
the positive return is when it finds it (return status 65537,
%RMS-S-NORMAL, normal successful completion) and not finding a file is
considered failure (returns 98962, %RMS-E-FNF). Any of the status messages
can normally have the severity bits changed to reflect the required
state, although in many (most?) cases this does not make sense! E.g. what
would status 545, "%SYSTEM-S-TAPEPOSLOST, magnetic tape position lost"
mean?

Unfortunately message 1 is the exception. Perhaps it shouldn't be.

>                                             No,
>it would hav been better if there were one message,
>	%SYSTEM-x-COMPLETION, completion

I think I finally understand! What you are saying is that the text
of status 1 (SS$_NORMAL) should be just "completion" and it should
not be specially handled, so that one could use...

    exit(1)    %SYSTEM-S-COMPLETION, completion   Successul end.

    exit(3)    %SYSTEM-I-COMPLETION, completion   Ended with information(?)

    exit(0)    %SYSTEM-W-COMPLETION, completion   ...with warnings

    exit(2)    %SYSTEM-E-COMPLETION, completion   ...with errors

    exit(4)    %SYSTEM-F-COMPLETION, completion   ...with severe errors!

While this does make some kind of sense, the whole point of the status
return mechanism is to return *USEFUL* information to the caller/user.
So if the program is not going to return simple success (exit(1)) then
it ought to have some additional information and should use a status with
some appropriate information in it!

--
