In article <40bn8l$a1c@caip.rutgers.edu>, caip.rutgers.edu!halasz writes:
=It seems to me that message 0, successfull completion, and message
=4, abort, are flip sides of the same thing, completion.  Were it
=not better, if message 0 were completion, and success or failure
=were shown by $SEVERITY?  I so believ.

Suppose you'd tell us where you find ABORT to be message 4?  As a system
service status, SS$_ABORT is 44, not 4.  That means that any value from 40 to
47 is "%SYSTEM-x-ABORT, abort", where x is W for 40, S for 41, E for 42, I for
43, F for 44, and ? for 45-47.  Similarly, messages 1-7 are
"%SYSTEM-x-NORMAL, normal successful completion", while 0 is
"%NONAME-W-NOMSG, Message number 00000000".  Note:  The C RTL, in a fit of
accomodation to Unix, converts an exit status of zero to 1.
