In article <1995Aug11.105442@genrad.co.uk>, dongray@genrad.co.uk (Derek Dongray) writes:
=This is not true. There are many reasons why one might want to return
=different levels of "abort":
=
=   exit(41)    %SYSTEM-S-ABORT, abort   The user requested an operation
=                                        be aborted rather than exiting
=                                        normally, This was handled
=                                        successfully.
=
=   exit(43)    %SYSTEM-I-ABORT, abort   Same as above, but since the
=                                        abort is not normally used we
=                                        flag it for information.
=
=   exit(40)    %SYSTEM-W-ABORT, abort   Everythings's probably OK, but
=                                        the user should be warned that
=                                        aborting is not recommended
=
=   exit(42)    %SYSTEM-E-ABORT, abort   The program should not have
=                                        been aborted. Something is wrong.
=
=   exit(44)    %SYSTEM-F-ABORT, abort   Something is seriously wrong!

Actually, my take on the severities is a bit different:
	0  Warning:	  Whatever was done apparently did the right thing, but
			  perhaps not what you expected.  For example, if you
			  attempt to delete a non-existent file, you get the
			  right result:  The file you specified isn't there. 
			  But perhaps that's not quite what you intended;
	1  Success:	  Whatever was done apparently did both the right and
			  the expected thing;
	2  Error:	  The right thing wasn't done, but you MAY be able to
			  continue as if it had been, and so attempting isn't
			  terribly likely to get you into immediate serious
			  trouble.
	3  Informational: The right and expected thing was done, but you asked
			  (perhaps by default) for the program to tell you what
			  it was doing.  See, for example, DEFINE/LOG vs.
			  DEFINE/NOLOG when you supersede a previously defined
			  logical name.
	4  Severe error:  The right thing wasn't done, and it would almost
			  certainly be a mistake to continue as if it had been.

=Of course, since the routine to find a file is looking for a named file,
=the positive return is when it finds it (return status 65537,
=%RMS-S-NORMAL, normal successful completion) and not finding a file is
=considered failure (returns 98962, %RMS-E-FNF). Any of the status messages
=can normally have the severity bits changed to reflect the required
=state, although in many (most?) cases this does not make sense! E.g. what
=would status 545, "%SYSTEM-S-TAPEPOSLOST, magnetic tape position lost"
=mean?

It could mean that your tape exercising program successfully found a way to
make the tape fail.
