In article <40g89i$h27@acmex.gatech.edu>, Steve Shepard wrote:

>If the code is compiled using the standard F77 command, the results
>obtained when runing the program are NOT ALWAYS CORRECT.  In one code, the
>results are correct with one loop of execution and incorrect on the next
>loop.  In this same code, the results are repeatable.  That is,  the Nth
>loop always gives the same, incorrect, answer.
>
>If the code is compiled with OPTIMIZATION OFF (using -g option), the
>results obtained when running the program are ALWAYS CORRECT.

He set his posting for replies to go back to the poster, and I have sent him
a more detailed answer, but I did want others to know that I had responded and
to suggest what might be the problem.  Here is part of what I wrote:

 One common programming error which can cause problems like this is violation
 of the Fortran 77 standard's prohibition against aliasing of dummy arguments
 and/or common.  One way this often happens is to pass the same array or
 overlapping portions of the same array as two separate arguments to the same
 routine.  The compiler assumes by default that you don't do this, and some
 significant optimizations take advantage of that knowledge.

Unfortunately, without having seen the source code, I can only guess, but if
the above is the problem, compiling with the -assume dummy_aliases switch
will disable the optimizations which depend on the absence of dummy argument
aliasing (storage association).

