>=standard (and some time after :-) the VMS C RTL couldn't care less about
>=the Unix conventions WRT exit status codes.
>
>Hmmm.  I thought the ANSI standard required that a successful exit status be
>defined by keyword.  The exit() function in the VAX C RTL converted zero to one
>before the ANSI standard was adopted.  return, on the other hand, left the
>value untouched.

The ANSI standard guarantees that returning a value from (the initial
invocation of) main() has the same effect as calling exit() with that
value as argument.  This is what DEC C does nowadays.  ANSI also
guarantees that 0 and EXIT_SUCCESS have the same effect when passed to
exit(), but it doesn't require that EXIT_SUCCESS is defined as 0 (and
DEC C for OpenVMS doesn't define EXIT_SUCCESS as 0).

Dan
