Michael A. Passineau (mikep@mfa.com) wrote:
: Hello,

: I do not know if this is the right forum for this question
: so please excuse my ignorance!

Excused.
Your lease is a legal document which must apply in this case unless
both parties agree to an amendment. If it specifies upgrades then there would
be a clause spelling out the terms. In the absence of a clause to upgrades
would be permissable unless both parties agreed to it (and an appropriate
clause added to the agreement spelling out new terms and conditions).

Many times people lease equipment to avoid being "stuck" with purchased
equipment which is obsolete. In the case of PCs, "obsolete" can sometimes
be measured in weeks or months.

There is nothing barring you from trying to re-negotiate your lease agreement.
However, the leasing company may not want the return of an "obsolete: piece
of equipment since the street value of such equipment may not cover the
actual cost left on the books ("upside-down").

Good luck.
