In article <Pine.A32.3.91.950814125953.67453A-100000@unix1.sncc.lsu.edu>, Thiagarajan Ganesh <cethia@unix1.sncc.lsu.edu> writes:

>Q) How do we access and change the partition information on the
>   disk so that the entire disk space could be mounted on a 
>   single partition ? In a SUN system it was very easy. I
>   tried a lot of things here but it did not work?

1.  Use the C partition.  That should be the entire disk.

2.  The missing space is probably the B partition at 128 MB.

If the disk hasn't been labeled, then the default partition
table for a supported disk can be found in the rz(7) manual
page.  For unsupported disks the default is:

A = 64 MB
B = 128 MB
C = entire disk
G = C - ( A + B )

If the disk has been labeled the default partition will be
written to the disk.  To look at the label use the disklabel(8)
command.  If you want change the partition table use the
disklabel command with the -e option.

