In article <40q6qo$jb6@grape.epix.net>, dpeasley@news.epix.net (Async User) writes:
|I have three VAX 8800 systems and one VAX 6440 which all use the DEBNA
|addapter for network traffic.  All systems are running VMS.  I zero the
|NCP line counters each night, but every day these four systems get over
|1000 errors in the 'SYSTEM BUFFER UNAVAILABLE' entry.  The NCP manual
|says that it can be any!!! buffer between the BNA and the user buffers.
|
|We have tried to increase every buffer that we'vew identified, but it
|hasn't helped.  The other systems in the cluster are using SVA, MNA, and
|EWA type devices and do not show this problem.
|
|Is there a mod or upgrade to the DEBNA adapters ???

I don't know if there any BI adapter less bad than the DEBNA.
There's not a whole lot you can do about your "system buffer
unavailable" problem - this means that the Ethernet driver is
getting data in faster than it can do anything about them -
presumably because of other high-IPL code running.

You might try dropping your advertised window size - say, if
doing mainly DECnet - by cutting the PIPELINE QUOTA to 4032
(a value that I found worked well, though others have different
opinions.)  If doing TCP, then maybe cut your receive window
size to 4KB.

You might also try sniffing your Ether to see if these "system
buffer unavailable" events correspond to broadcast storms
or somesuch.

Regards,

Aaron

