In <409n4u$3j4@cortaro.math.arizona.edu> hays@math.arizona.edu (Mark Hays) writes:

>I think I may have found a bug in the DEC f77 compiler.
>
>From the f77(1) manpage:
> "Arguments with the suffix .F are interpreted as FORTRAN source pro-
> grams which must be processed by the C preprocessor (cpp(1)) before
> being compiled."
>
>From the cpp(1) manpage:
> "The cpp preprocessor accepts C++-style end of line comments (//).  This
> means that everything following the two slashes (//) to the end of the line
> in which they appear is considered as a comment."
>
>Since there doesn't seem to be a way to turn off the
>removal of C++ comments, may I assume that one should
>never concatenate strings (with //) in DEC f77 .F
>files?

Have you actually tried to compile a .F file which contains // with f77
and had problems?  I have only f90 installed and I never had this problem
because the compiler does the right thing: it invokes cpp with whatever
magic options are required to disable the C++ comments:

    ues5:~/tmp 499> cat foo.F
	   print *, "foo" // "bar"
	   end
    ues5:~/tmp 500> f90 foo.F
    ues5:~/tmp 501> ./a.out
     foobar

I complained to DEC about the (broken, IMHO) _default_ behaviour of cpp
WRT C++ style comments (it breaks utilities like xrdb if you have URL's
in your .Xdefaults), but their answer was:

    Here is the feedback we received from Engineering concerning the "cpp"
    behaviour which makes you feel unhappy.

    After careful reflection, they decided to leave the "C++ comment style"
    functionnality of "cpp" unchanged.

Dan
