In article <3vu1c4$4e9@amy4.Stanford.EDU> ahmadpk@leland.stanford.edu (Bilal Ahmad) writes:

Bilal> We own a DECAlphaServer 2000 4/200 machine, which at the time
Bilal> of purchase came with 4 2.1GB (DEC RZ28-VA, 3.5in) mounted with
Bilal> snap-in carriers inside a StorageWorks housing which can hold a
Bilal> total of 8 disks.

The problem you are most likely going to run into is that your system
doesn't have the other four slots hooked upto the SCSI controller.
Does your system have a CDROM in it as well?  If so, then you really
have two options.

1. Buy a KZPAA-AA PCI SCSI card and proper cable to hook the card to
   the other half of the storageworks shelf.  This is what DEC will
   tell you to do.  We just bought one of those cards for our 2100
   4/200 for $260 and installed it with very few problems.  This is
   what I would suggest you do since it will give you future expansion
   room, and isn't all that expensive to do.

2. The cheap, but NOT supported way is to extend the current SCSI
   cabling to include the other half of the SW shelf.  This is a
   supported configuration if all you have is that one shelf hooked to
   a SCSI controller.  If you have other devices in the chain, then
   it's a no no since you can have two devices thinking they are the
   same SCSI bus address, which is bad. 

Bilal> At this time we need to add another disk. I was wondering if I
Bilal> can add a third party disk in the StorageWorks housing (an
Bilal> internal SCSI disk). More specifically, is the StorageWorks
Bilal> cabinet that we have is a standard that other vendors (other
Bilal> than DEC) can make their drives fit to.  Or do I have to get an
Bilal> externally housed drive, if it's a third party item?

What you need to do is buy the third party disk, then install it in
DEC's generic 3.5" fixed disk carrier.  Cost's about $150 for the
carrier, which is a bit much.  The part number is SWXBA-BA.  We have
four Barracuda 2s on our 2100 and they're running quite well.

Bilal> Whatever the answer, do people have suggestions for vendors in
Bilal> the San Francisco bay area, who sell drives of the type/quality
Bilal> (I am currently thinking of a Barracuda 4 from Seagate, it's a
Bilal> 4GB, 1M cache, 7200 rpm) and those who can provide a quick
Bilal> replacement (a day or two max) upon failure.

Any of the large DEC VARs should be able to help you, even if they
aren't in the SF area.  I'm at home so I don't have any addresses handy.

