In article <1995Aug8.094324@bbs.ug.eds.com>,
Eric R Showalter (J.L. French Corp.) <showalter@bbs.ug.eds.com> wrote:
>The problem is that the DEC2100 is *not* and old DEC system but a new one. The

As I stated before, the DECstation 2100 is a reduced clock speed
version of the DECstation 3100.  It is also known as the PMIN (the
DECstation 3100 is the PMAX).   Believe me, I've got 3 of the
doorstops running in my department.   They make nice X terminals.


>3100 is the old risc-based DECStation and the 2100 is the new Alpha-based
>DECStation (or MicroVAX, VAXStation and new Alpha VAX , if your into that OS).

Both machines are based on RISC technology.  The DECstation 2100 has a
single MIPS R2000 processor running at 12.5Mhz or so.  The AlphaServer
2100 is the machine you're talking about.  It has up to 4 Alpha
processors, either EV4 or EV5, running at up to 275Mhz (EV5), or 250Mhz
(EV5).

There is no such thing as an 'Alpha VAX.'  That's like saying a
'PowerPC 68040' or a 'MIPS R8000 486.'

>I believe that the 2100 is in the AlphaServer family not the AlphaStation, but
>am not for sure.

The real problem is that Digital decided to confuse everybody by
re-using the product number 2100.  So unless somebody specifies
AlphaServer 2100 or DECstation 2100, you need to ask what 2100 they're
talking about or simply infer it from context.

Drew

