In article <3vjm4n$mb@chnews.ch.intel.com>, pitterle@sedona.intel.com (Matt Pitterle -FT-~) writes:
>I have some DEC 3100's running ULTRIX-32 V3.1.

V3.0 was released in April 1989.  V3.1 was probably released later
that same year.

>It seems that when doing a df on large  (greater than 2GB)
>mounted filesystems the output is incorrect.
>I looked through some FAQ's available but had no luck.

Since you can have a *local* file system larger than 1 GB,
is it safe to assume that these large file system are NFS
mounted?

Using a slightly more modern version of ULTRIX; V4.4 on a
DECstation 5000/240:

% df /mnt/OSF
Filesystem        Total    kbytes   kbytes   %     
node              kbytes   used     free     used  Mounted on
mennoly:/mnt/OSF  7975309 5366257 1811521    75%   /mnt/OSF

On the server:

% df -k /mnt/OSF
Filesystem       kbytes        used       avail capacity  Mounted on
/dev/ds0c       7975309     5366257     1811521    75%    /mnt/OSF

So, V4.4 seems to handle a large file system reasonably.  However,
if I use V4.3:

% df /mnt/OSF
Filesystem   Total    kbytes   kbytes   %     
node         kbytes   used     free     used  Mounted on
mennoly:/mnt 3781005 1171953 1811521    39%   /mnt/OSF

>
>Any direction?

Upgrade to V4.4 or write your own version of df(1) that doesn't
multiply the block count by the block size before converting
down to KB.

