[snip]

>Now whilst I can change all our code to not refer to errno in this structure, 
>I feel that the DEC 'C' errno.h does not have correct behaviour in it. Is the 
>DEC 'C' compiler in error (my K&R 2nd Ed does refer to errno as an integer, 
>but that's all), or am I not allowed to do this?

DEC are allowed to do this. Paraphrasing the standard ("Reserved Identifiers" 
and "Errors <errno.h>")

  -   when <errno.h> is included, the identifier 'errno' is a reserved
      identifier.

  -   errno may be a macro; regardless it must result in a modifiable
      lvalue of type 'int'

They even give an example in a footnote not unlike DEC's implmentation.

Claude.


