X-Deja-AN: 107282425
distribution: world
references: <3vjd04$fue@giga.bga.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Scotland Ltd
reply-to: gordon.mcnab@ayo.mts.dec.com
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

In article <3vjd04$fue@giga.bga.com> rou@cs.utexas.edu (Rong C. Ou) wrote:
> Hi,
> 
>   We have a DEC AlphaStation 200 4/233 running OSF/1 3.2. I want to be 
> able to mount a CD-ROM from a user's account, but when I "su root", all I 
> got was "You do not have permission to su root". So I had to log out and 
> log in as root to do that. Any suggestions on how to do this the easy 
> way?
> 
>   Any help is appreciated.
> 
> Rong
> rou@cs.utexas.edu


You could add the user to the system group in /etc/group. For example:

#cat /etc/group
system:*:0:root,me
users:*:15:me

That will allow an su to be done from that user account. Although, this
does allow the user to access files with a group id of system as well.
You may not want to do this!


